WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.053
"Hey, Grandpa. What are these toy soldiers?"

2
00:00:06.054 --> 00:00:09.722
Laurie was looking through an archaeology book in Grandpa's study.

3
00:00:09.723 --> 00:00:14.224
"Those aren't toys, Laurie," Grandpa said, looking at the picture.

4
00:00:14.225 --> 00:00:18.351
"Those are life-size clay soldiers!

5
00:00:18.352 --> 00:00:27.709
And they're on duty for eternity, guarding the tomb of the first emperor of one of the oldest civilizations in the world—China!

6
00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:30.103
Take a look at the next photo."

7
00:00:30.104 --> 00:00:37.073
Laurie turned the page and saw an archaeologist carefully dusting the top of one clay soldier's head.

8
00:00:37.074 --> 00:00:41.576
The archaeologist and the statue were nearly the same size!

9
00:00:41.577 --> 00:00:47.224
"They are big!" she exclaimed. "And there are so many of them!

10
00:00:47.225 --> 00:00:55.273
Who was this emperor? He must have been really important to have all these soldiers made just for him."

11
00:00:55.274 --> 00:01:00.320
"Well, as emperor, you can pretty much get whatever you want.

12
00:01:00.321 --> 00:01:11.222
But, yes, he was important. Known as Qin Shihuangdi, he was the first emperor of China and the emperor of the Qin dynasty,

13
00:01:11.223 --> 00:01:17.541
which lasted from 221 BC to 207

14
00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:24.317
BC. In fact, the romanized name ‘China’ is believed to come from the Qin

15
00:01:24.318 --> 00:01:37.245
dynasty. Chinese civilization had been around since about 1600 BC, but Shihuangdi was the first ruler to unite the land into an empire ruled by one

16
00:01:37.246 --> 00:01:42.268
government. He was a cruel leader, but he did a lot for

17
00:01:42.269 --> 00:01:51.236
China. He ordered that the same measuring, writing, and money systems be used throughout the empire, which helped

18
00:01:51.237 --> 00:02:00.797
trade. He also organized the building of many roads, canals, and the longest structure ever built—the Great Wall of

19
00:02:00.798 --> 00:02:04.365
China." "How was he cruel?" Laurie

20
00:02:04.366 --> 00:02:12.107
asked. "Building these enormous projects, especially the Great Wall, required a lot of hard

21
00:02:12.108 --> 00:02:16.174
work. Shihuangdi forced his people to work for

22
00:02:16.175 --> 00:02:20.815
him. They often worked long hours on difficult

23
00:02:20.816 --> 00:02:25.261
jobs. Many actually died during the building of the Great

24
00:02:25.262 --> 00:02:32.549
Wall. He also believed that in order to rule a large empire, a ruler must make strict

25
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:41.397
laws. One law punished scholars who thought or wrote about things the emperor believed were harmful or not useful to his

26
00:02:41.398 --> 00:02:47.902
empire. These included literature, philosophy, and of course criticism of his

27
00:02:47.903 --> 00:02:57.310
rule. He also famously burned all history books because he thought they might make people question his authority. Can you believe

28
00:02:57.311 --> 00:03:03.550
it?" "Wow. Without history books how would we learn about the past?" Laurie

29
00:03:03.551 --> 00:03:09.965
asked. "Well, written history helps, but there are other ways of discovering the

30
00:03:09.966 --> 00:03:20.832
past. Archaeology, for example! Luckily for Chinese historians, Shihuangdi's style of rule didn't last

31
00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:28.614
long. Soon after his death in 210 BC, his empire collapsed and the Han dynasty

32
00:03:28.615 --> 00:03:33.458
began. "Han rulers looked to China's past for ideas about the

33
00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:38.122
future. They returned to the ideas of

34
00:03:38.123 --> 00:03:44.547
Confucius. He was an ancient scholar who believed that rulers should lead by good example

35
00:03:44.548 --> 00:03:51.071
only—not by forcing people to obey strict laws—and that people should respect their elders. His

36
00:03:51.072 --> 00:04:00.364
philosophy later spread throughout Asia, and his influence on Asian culture and society can still be seen today.

37
00:04:00.365 --> 00:04:06.295
Unlike the first emperor, Han rulers prized scholars.

38
00:04:06.296 --> 00:04:15.642
In fact, during the Han dynasty, a system was created to make sure that only the best scholars would receive government jobs.

39
00:04:15.643 --> 00:04:21.873
Anyone who wanted to work for the government had to take an extremely difficult test.

40
00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:26.269
China had the first government full of scholars!

41
00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:38.228
And nearly two thousand years later, this practice of making government employees pass tests was adopted by American and European governments too.

42
00:04:38.229 --> 00:04:43.868
"The Han rulers continued to worry about foreign invaders, however,

43
00:04:43.869 --> 00:04:51.307
so they kept building Shihuangdi's Great Wall and created more roads for soldiers to travel on.

44
00:04:51.308 --> 00:04:55.487
With improved roads, trade continued to grow.

45
00:04:55.488 --> 00:05:03.964
The famous Silk Road, one of the greatest international trading routes in history, developed during the Han dynasty.

46
00:05:03.965 --> 00:05:14.721
Goods such as silk, tea, pottery, and spices traveled out of China to India, the Middle East, Russia, and Europe along that route.

47
00:05:14.722 --> 00:05:21.408
The exchange of ideas and culture between the East and the West also began along the Silk Road."

48
00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:25.213
"Was the road made out of silk?" Laurie asked.

49
00:05:25.214 --> 00:05:31.781
Grandpa chuckled. "No, but a lot of silk traveled along it.

50
00:05:31.782 --> 00:05:36.287
Silk, you see, was invented by the Chinese.

51
00:05:36.288 --> 00:05:47.523
Evidence shows they were making it as early as 1300 BC, but no one outside of China knew about it until centuries later.

52
00:05:47.524 --> 00:05:54.769
Once people of other countries touched the beautiful fabric, they loved it and wanted it for themselves.

53
00:05:54.770 --> 00:06:00.016
But the Chinese were determined to keep their silk-making process a secret.

54
00:06:00.017 --> 00:06:04.329
If foreigners wanted silk, they'd have to pay or trade.

55
00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:09.502
So silk was carried out of China along the Silk Road.

56
00:06:09.503 --> 00:06:16.406
"Actually many things that were invented in ancient China would later travel to the West.

57
00:06:16.407 --> 00:06:23.797
There's paper, for one. And the wheelbarrow, the compass, and the first machine to detect earthquakes!

58
00:06:23.798 --> 00:06:32.527
The Han dynasty valued knowledge and learning, and they encouraged scientists to study and develop new

59
00:06:32.528 --> 00:06:41.114
things. They also valued the arts. The Han dynasty is known for its beautiful and unique pottery, poetry, and

60
00:06:41.115 --> 00:06:46.763
sculpture. Chinese civilization really flourished during this

61
00:06:46.764 --> 00:06:52.013
period. The dynasty lasted longer than any other—about four hundred

62
00:06:52.014 --> 00:06:59.405
years—and the Chinese consider it so important that even today they call themselves people of the Han."

63
00:06:59.406 --> 00:07:05.141
"Laurie?" Mom suddenly called. "Have you seen my red silk blouse?

64
00:07:05.142 --> 00:07:08.156
I want to wear it when I go out tonight."

65
00:07:08.157 --> 00:07:13.773
"You mean your Chinese red silk blouse?" Laurie shouted back.

66
00:07:13.774 --> 00:07:17.341
"No, I don't have a Chinese blouse, Laurie."

67
00:07:17.342 --> 00:07:20.432
Now Mom's voice was coming from outside the door.

68
00:07:20.433 --> 00:07:23.333
"What are you guys talking about in there anyway?"

69
00:07:23.334 --> 00:07:30.132
"Oh, all kinds of things! Silk, clay soldiers, a great wall!" Laurie answered.

70
00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:33.203
She turned to Grandpa and handed him back his book.

71
00:07:33.204 --> 00:07:37.269
"I'd better go help Mom find her blouse. Thanks again, Grandpa.

72
00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:44.251
See you in another place at another time!"

